> The opencode-mpm plugin handles session-start wake-context injection
> automatically via the `experimental.chat.system.transform` hook. This
> AGENTS.md section adds the **behavioral** layer that the hook can't
> reasonably inject (handoff discipline, persist-during-work, recovery).
> Edit the canonical protocol at
> `~/.mpm/agent_installation/mpm-agent-protocol.md` rather than this section
> for behavioral changes.

## MPM behavioral contract — host-independent

MPM is the persistent agent substrate for cross-session continuity on
this machine. The full behavioral contract is canonical at
`~/.mpm/agent_installation/mpm-agent-protocol.md`. The non-negotiable invariants
that this host adapter inherits:

1. **Wake on session start.** Already handled automatically by the
   opencode-mpm plugin (`fetchWakeContext` in
   `src/index.ts`). You do not need to call `read_wake_context` manually
   at session start.

2. **Persist during work, not only at the end.** Use the appropriate MPM
   tool (`mpm_memory`, `mpm_decisions`, `mpm_lessons`, `mpm_topics`,
   `mpm_references`) for any durable knowledge a future session would
   otherwise have to rediscover. The plugin proxies these calls through
   `mpm call` via the `buildProvenanceEnv` shim so provenance is captured.

3. **Skill discovery before reinventing.** When the current task
   context suggests a previously learned procedure would apply, query
   `mpm_context(action: "proactive_recall_hint", params: {
   conversation_text: "<recent task summary>" })` and read each
   surfaced skill via `mpm_skills(action: "read", params: {name})`.
   The wake payload already includes a bounded `<available_skills>`
   catalogue — discovery adds a context-driven filter on top. Don't
   auto-scan every turn; invoke when context suggests a remembered
   procedure would apply. Reactive catalog fallback via
   `mpm_skills(action: "list", params: {scope: "all"})`.

4. **Handoff before genuine session closure.** Before sending a turn
   that closes the session, run the handoff check. If substantial work
   has occurred since the last handoff, write it via
   `mpm_handoff(action: "write", params: {session_id, summary, state,
   commitments, open_questions, note})`. Use
   `mpm_scratchpad(action: "flush"|"read"|"discard"|"promote",
   params: {session_id, thesis, supporting}) for in-session volatile
   working state. Wake is how future-me starts; handoff is how
   future-me receives the previous session.

   Mid-session acknowledgements (`ok`, `thanks`, `ty`, `ack`) are NOT
   session-closing. Do not write a handoff on every chat ack — write one
   when substantial work has happened or a session-closing trigger fires.

5. **MPM is the source of truth for cross-session continuity.** Anything
   that should survive across sessions goes to MPM, not just transient
   conversation context.

6. **Recovery / fallback.** If the plugin is disabled or unavailable
   (e.g., the underlying `mpm` binary fails or the plugin is removed),
   fall back to the CLI path:
   `mpm call <tool> --payload '{"action":"<op>","params":{...}}'`
   Do not abandon persistence when the preferred integration breaks.

## OpenCode-specific notes

The opencode-mpm plugin's `experimental.chat.system.transform` hook
handles session-start wake-context injection automatically — you do not
need to call `read_wake_context` manually at the start of sessions.

The plugin stamps provenance for every MPM call:
`framework_name=opencode`, with `model_name` and `invocation_id` set per
call. No further action is required for attribution.

Host-specific recovery details (OpenCode plugin lifecycle, system-prompt
transformation quirks, etc.) are documented in the plugin's README.
Refer there before assuming a generic MPM recovery pattern will work
under OpenCode.
